  "preferred_feature": "Emphysema on imaging",
  "rationale": "Emphysema on imaging has stronger and more consistent evidence for predicting differences in treatment benefit. Across the overlapping analyses, it showed nonzero penalized treatment-interaction coefficients in 3/3 analyses, positive univariable R-learner validation R-loss gains in 3/3 analyses with magnitudes of 0.004, 0.006, and 0.003, and positive causal-forest permutation R-loss increases in 3/3 analyses with magnitudes of 0.003, 0.004, and 0.002. This combination is important because the R-loss gains and causal-forest permutation increases directly assess whether the variable improves prediction of treatment-effect heterogeneity after accounting for treatment and outcome predictions. Its univariable interaction p-values were also mostly nominal significant, though not adjusted for multiplicity. Serum creatinine had two nominally significant interaction p-values in the univariable screen, but its stronger heterogeneity evidence was weaker and often absent: only 1/3 penalized interaction analyses were nonzero, 0/3 R-learner validation gains were positive, and only 1/3 causal-forest permutation R-loss increases were positive. Because the treatment-effect analyses use probability differences and the R-loss/causal-forest results are on comparable scales, emphysema's consistently positive validation improvements indicate better support for modifying treatment benefit than the creatinine findings, which are dominated by null or negative R-loss results and limited causal-forest support. Redundancy between the variables is unavailable, so no inference can be made that either variable substitutes for the other."
